// The starship from the original series, built part by part from the plans and the studio model.
//
//     import 'enterprise' { Enterprise }
//
//     object Enterprise(289)
//
// | `Enterprise` | The whole ship: saucer, dorsal, secondary hull, pylons and nacelles, in detail. |
// | `EnterpriseSaucer`, `EnterpriseSecondaryHull` | Her two halves, in the plans' own frame. |
// | `EnterpriseNacelle`, `EnterpriseImpulseEngines` | Two of the parts they are built from. |
//
// **The first number is a length**, in **meters**, and she **faces `+X`** with her origin in her own
// middle -- the conventions of the [spacecraft](#spacecraft), for the same reason: she rests on nothing,
// so an origin in the middle is what lets `rotate` turn her about herself.  289 is the published figure,
// and `long` scales her whole.  `variant` is taken for the same uniformity and does nothing; there was
// one of her.
//
// -- Where the shape comes from -----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Franz Joseph's *Star Trek Blueprints* (1975), his Booklet of General Plans: the saucer's profile and
// cutaway, the secondary hull's profile, top, bottom and rear views and the cutaway of its nose, the
// dorsal's section, and the nacelle's profile, top and bottom.  Every part was traced from scans of
// those sheets, which measure 580.4 pixels to one of the model's units -- the reason a few comments
// below give a fit in pixels.
//
// **Where the plans and the studio model disagree, the studio model wins.**  The control reactor's
// recess and the reactor in it, the intercoolers' height, the inset webs under them, the vents, the
// homing beacon, and the stern -- the hood round the hangar doors, the ribbed doors and the light above
// them -- are taken from photographs of the original model, not from the plans: the Star Trek Starship
// Enterprise Studio Model (A19740668000, gift of Paramount Pictures Inc.) in the Smithsonian's National
// Air and Space Museum, whose photographs are at
// https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/model-starship-enterprise-television-show-star-trek/nasm_A19740668000
//
// -- Her own frame ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The parts are written in the plans' units with **+X aft**, the saucer's axis on Y and its rim at
// y = 0, so the saucer is 12.625 across and the whole ship 29.61 long from the saucer's rim to the
// nacelles' end caps.  `Enterprise` turns her about to face +X, moves her middle to the origin and scales
// her to meters, so a scene never sees this frame; it is here for a scene that wants one part alone.
//
// -- Her colors ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **The hull plating has no material of its own**, so it takes what the scene gives the object: a
// material on `object Enterprise(...)` repaints the plating and leaves alone every part that names its
// color -- the deflector's orange, the copper of the inner cups and the antenna, the bronze of the
// Bussard domes, the sensor domes, the darker gray of the dorsal rib on the saucer, the gold of the strips
// under it, the dark of the unlit windows, and the matte gray of the vanes, the reactor's recess, the
// vents, the grooves, the nacelles' hoods, the pylons' grilles, the tabs' vents and the wall behind the
// hangar doors.  Unpainted, she is the renderer's default gray, 0.40, which shows as 0.66 with gamma on.
//
// **A glow is a material at `ambient 1`** -- the impulse ports, the homing beacon, the bow light, the
// running lights above and below, the lighted panels, the red lamp on the strip, the hangar's light, the
// lights along its deck and the lit windows -- and its color is the one it shows under one white light.  Every light
// adds its own ambient, in proportion to its own color: a second light at full strength makes a glow
// twice as bright, and a dim fill adds a little.  The lit windows glow a yellow warm white, as the studio
// model's photograph.
//
// **Her markings are painted over whatever plating she is given.**  They are decals in a material that
// inherits -- `material inherited { decal { .. } }` -- so a material on the object repaints the plating
// and leaves the lettering, the pennants and the outlined doors, panels and hatches where they are.
//
// -- Her lettering ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The lettering is laid out from FJ's views of the saucer, the secondary hull and the nacelle, sized to
// the studio model where the two differ, and set in "Federation Starfleet Hull 23rd", a face whose own
// name table credits it as AmarilloUSAF, (c) 1994 Tom C. Lai: shareware, whose terms prohibit passing it
// on, so it is not part of this repository.  To letter her in it, import a
// copy of your own into the font catalog:
//
//     RayTracer fonts --import 'Federation Starfleet Hull 23rd' path/to/Federation_Starfleet_Hull_23rd.ttf
//
// Without it the lettering falls back to Orbitron, which Google Fonts carries and the renderer fetches
// for itself.  Orbitron is the wider of the two, so the lines run a little longer in it.

// -- Her parts, in the plans' frame -------------------------------------------------------------------

EnterpriseImpulseEngines = union {
    difference {
        union {
            intersection {
                tapered extrusion {
                    taper 0.777778
                    path {
                        // drawn 1.125 times the old base, about this half's own taper axis, so that the taper
                        // (0.875/1.125 over the height of 1) passes through exactly the old base at y = 0 and
                        // the old top at y = 0.5; below y = 0 it carries on at the same lean to meet the rim
                        move to 0.694443, 0
                        line to 2.693748, 0
                        curve 2.800538, 0, 2.887108, -0.08657 to 2.887108, -0.193359
                        curve 2.887108, -0.300149, 2.800538, -0.386719 to 2.693748, -0.386719
                        line to 0.694443, -0.386719
                        close
                    }
                    min Y -0.5
                    max Y 0.5
                    translate [-0.894443, 0, 0]
                }
                cube {
                    // keeps x >= 0 only: the half is clipped where it meets its twin
                    scale [2, 1, 1]
                    translate [2, 0.25, -0.17]
                }
            }
            intersection {
                tapered extrusion {
                    taper 0.777778
                    path {
                        // drawn 1.125 times the old base, about this half's own taper axis, so that the taper
                        // (0.875/1.125 over the height of 1) passes through exactly the old base at y = 0 and
                        // the old top at y = 0.5; below y = 0 it carries on at the same lean to meet the rim
                        move to -0.694443, 0
                        line to -2.693748, 0
                        curve -2.800538, 0, -2.887108, -0.08657 to -2.887108, -0.193359
                        curve -2.887108, -0.300149, -2.800538, -0.386719 to -2.693748, -0.386719
                        line to -0.694443, -0.386719
                        close
                    }
                    min Y -0.5
                    max Y 0.5
                    translate [0.894443, 0, 0]
                }
                cube {
                    // keeps x <= 0 only: the half is clipped where it meets its twin
                    scale [2, 1, 1]
                    translate [-2, 0.25, -0.17]
                }
            }
        }

        // The exhaust ports: two plain rectangles cut into the aft face (the extrusion's small end, at
        // y = 0.5), their outer edges 0.745543 out from the middle and their inner edges 0.14 out, so
        // each is 0.605543 wide (22.4% of the face's 2.702171) with 0.28 between them;
        // inset 0.05 from the face's top and bottom edges, and 0.07 deep.  Each cutter pokes 0.02 out
        // past the face so the two never share a surface.  The cutters take the hull's material, so
        // the walls of each recess shade like the rest of the block and the depth can be seen.
        cube {
            scale [0.302771, 0.045, 0.100391]
            translate [0.442771, 0.475, -0.150391]
        }
        cube {
            scale [0.302771, 0.045, 0.100391]
            translate [-0.442771, 0.475, -0.150391]
        }
    }

    // The glow at the back of each port: a thin plate a little larger than the opening, so its edges are
    // buried in the recess walls, with its face 0.004 proud of the recess floor.  A flat, self-lit
    // yellow-orange, its own color under one white light (see the header on glows and lights).
    cube {
        scale [0.307771, 0.012, 0.105391]
        translate [0.442771, 0.422, -0.150391]
        material {
            pigment [1, 0.5, 0.05]          // as it shows under one white light
            ambient 1
            diffuse 0
            specular 0
        }
    }
    cube {
        scale [0.307771, 0.012, 0.105391]
        translate [-0.442771, 0.422, -0.150391]
        material {
            pigment [1, 0.5, 0.05]          // as it shows under one white light
            ambient 1
            diffuse 0
            specular 0
        }
    }
    rotate X -90
    rotate Y -90
}

EnterpriseSaucer = union {
    // Her name and registration, across the top of the saucer forward of the bridge, as FJ's top view
    // draws them: both centered on the centerline, reading from starboard to port with the letters'
    // tops toward the stern.  The paths are in the font's own units, an em to a line, and each decal's
    // scale makes the capitals the height FJ draws them -- the hull face's capitals are 0.6 of an em.
    // FJ spaces his letters wider than the face does, so each line is kerned out to his spacing, and
    // reaches only the saucer's upper skin.
    material inherited {
        // The registration stands on an arc about the saucer's center, its feet toward the bow: FJ's
        // capitals run from radius 3.47 to 4.28, so 0.81 tall, 1.35 to the em, and the arc 3.170 ems
        // out.  Drawn from starboard round the bow to port, the center is on the path's left, where
        // the letters stand.  His letters' middles fall 51.4 degrees apart from the first C to the
        // last digit, which this face matches with 66 more to each pair.
        decal {
            path {
                text {
                    text 'NCC-1701'
                    font 'Federation Starfleet Hull 23rd', 'Orbitron'
                    layout {
                        along path { move to 0, 3.1704
                                     curve -1.7509, 3.1704, -3.1704, 1.7509 to -3.1704, 0
                                     curve -3.1704, -1.7509, -1.7509, -3.1704 to 0, -3.1704 }
                        horizontal position center
                    }
                    kerning {
                        kern 'N', 'C', 66  kern 'C', 'C', 66  kern 'C', '-', 66  kern '-', '1', 66
                        kern '1', '7', 66  kern '7', '0', 66  kern '0', '1', 66
                    }
                }
            }
            color [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]
            planar  min Y 0  max Y 0.6
            scale 1.35
        }
        // The name runs straight across, its feet at X = -2.437 and its capitals 0.230 tall, so 0.383
        // to the em and the line 6.357 ems forward.  Drawn from starboard to port, its left is aft.
        // FJ's "CONSTITUTION" runs 3.095 from its first letter to its last, which this face matches
        // with 147 more to each pair.  (The sheet is scanned 0.78 degrees askew, which is taken out
        // before measuring a straight line on it.)
        decal {
            path {
                text {
                    text 'U.S.S. ENTERPRISE'
                    font 'Federation Starfleet Hull 23rd', 'Orbitron'
                    layout {
                        along path { move to -6.3574, 12  line to -6.3574, -12 }
                        horizontal position center
                    }
                    kerning {
                        kern 'U', '.', 147  kern '.', 'S', 147  kern 'S', '.', 147  kern '.', ' ', 147
                        kern ' ', 'E', 147  kern 'E', 'N', 147  kern 'N', 'T', 147  kern 'T', 'E', 147
                        kern 'E', 'R', 147  kern 'R', 'P', 147  kern 'P', 'R', 147  kern 'R', 'I', 147
                        kern 'I', 'S', 147  kern 'S', 'E', 147
                    }
                }
            }
            color [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]
            planar  min Y 0  max Y 0.6
            scale 0.3833
        }
        // The registration underneath, along the beam on both sides, reading in from just inside the rim:
        // to port as FJ's bottom view draws it, and to starboard as the studio model carries it too,
        // turned half round so that its N is nearest the rim as well.  It is the studio model's size, which
        // is larger than FJ's, measured from the photograph of the starboard underside through the same
        // fitted camera as the landing gear doors: capitals 0.35 tall, 0.583 to the em, centered on the
        // beam -- so the baseline runs 0.175 off it, forward to port and aft to starboard, where the
        // letters' tops face the other way -- the N's outer edge 5.533 out, just inside the running
        // light, and the whole 2.398 long, which this face matches with 36 more to each pair.  Seen from
        // below, a decal comes out mirrored, so each is turned over; that also points its projection
        // down, so its reach is measured downward, taking in the underside beneath it (-0.40 to -0.75)
        // and nothing above.
        decal {
            path {
                text {
                    text 'NCC-1701'
                    font 'Federation Starfleet Hull 23rd', 'Orbitron'
                    layout { along path { move to -0.3000, 9.5857  line to -0.3000, 0 } }
                    kerning {
                        kern 'N', 'C', 36  kern 'C', 'C', 36  kern 'C', '-', 36  kern '-', '1', 36
                        kern '1', '7', 36  kern '7', '0', 36  kern '0', '1', 36
                    }
                }
            }
            color [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]
            planar  min Y 0.4  max Y 0.75
            scale 0.5833
            rotate X 180
        }
        decal {
            path {
                text {
                    text 'NCC-1701'
                    font 'Federation Starfleet Hull 23rd', 'Orbitron'
                    layout { along path { move to 0.3000, -9.5857  line to 0.3000, 0 } }
                    kerning {
                        kern 'N', 'C', 36  kern 'C', 'C', 36  kern 'C', '-', 36  kern '-', '1', 36
                        kern '1', '7', 36  kern '7', '0', 36  kern '0', '1', 36
                    }
                }
            }
            color [0.06, 0.06, 0.07]
            planar  min Y 0.4  max Y 0.75
            scale 0.5833
            rotate X 180
        }
        // The landing gear doors, as the studio model's makers are said to have called them: a long, curved
        // triangle flat on the underside either side, forward of the registration, its tip toward the rim
        // and its short base 2.86 out.  FJ does not draw them; they were placed from the photograph of the
        // starboard underside, by fitting a camera to it on four points of the ship and reading where its
        // corners and edges fall on the hull.  They are painted, not built -- the photograph shows a flat
        // panel with a dark seam round it -- and only their edges are painted, so the plating shows within:
        // a seam 0.04 wide just outside the outline, in a darker gray, and a border 0.05 wide just inside
        // it, in the dorsal rib's.  Each is a ring, an outline with an outline inside it, which the even-odd
        // rule turns into the band between the two.
        decal {
            path { move to -2.3393, -4.2590  curve -2.2190, -3.5590, -2.0086, -2.8729 to -1.7234, -2.2223  line to -1.1554, -2.6017  curve -1.5556, -3.1503, -2.0323, -3.6466 to -2.3393, -4.2590  close
                   move to -2.2710, -4.0710  curve -2.1319, -3.4565, -1.9593, -2.8545 to -1.7060, -2.2820  line to -1.2120, -2.6120  curve -1.5606, -3.0998, -1.9984, -3.5321 to -2.2710, -4.0710  close
                   move to -2.3393, 4.2590  curve -2.2190, 3.5590, -2.0086, 2.8729 to -1.7234, 2.2223  line to -1.1554, 2.6017  curve -1.5556, 3.1503, -2.0323, 3.6466 to -2.3393, 4.2590  close
                   move to -2.2710, 4.0710  curve -2.1319, 3.4565, -1.9593, 2.8545 to -1.7060, 2.2820  line to -1.2120, 2.6120  curve -1.5606, 3.0998, -1.9984, 3.5321 to -2.2710, 4.0710  close }
            color [0.18, 0.18, 0.18]           // the seam, 0.46 as it shows with gamma on
            planar  min Y 0.4  max Y 1.0
            rotate X 180
        }
        decal {
            path { move to -2.2710, -4.0710  curve -2.1319, -3.4565, -1.9593, -2.8545 to -1.7060, -2.2820  line to -1.2120, -2.6120  curve -1.5606, -3.0998, -1.9984, -3.5321 to -2.2710, -4.0710  close
                   move to -2.1431, -3.7496  curve -2.0241, -3.2751, -1.8783, -2.8062 to -1.6841, -2.3568  line to -1.2829, -2.6248  curve -1.5613, -3.0061, -1.8865, -3.3522 to -2.1431, -3.7496  close
                   move to -2.2710, 4.0710  curve -2.1319, 3.4565, -1.9593, 2.8545 to -1.7060, 2.2820  line to -1.2120, 2.6120  curve -1.5606, 3.0998, -1.9984, 3.5321 to -2.2710, 4.0710  close
                   move to -2.1431, 3.7496  curve -2.0241, 3.2751, -1.8783, 2.8062 to -1.6841, 2.3568  line to -1.2829, 2.6248  curve -1.5613, 3.0061, -1.8865, 3.3522 to -2.1431, 3.7496  close }
            color [0.2938, 0.2938, 0.2938]     // the dorsal rib's gray, 0.57 as it shows with gamma on
            planar  min Y 0.4  max Y 1.0
            rotate X 180
        }
    }
    lathe {
        path {
            move to 0, 0.46875
            quad -2.3362542, 0.46875 to -4.625, 0
            line to -6.3125, 0
    
            // the rim face: straight, raked back, dr/dh = 0.5156
            line to -6.0444, -0.5200
    
            // the underside: it undercuts behind the rim, cresting at h = -0.465 near r = 4.3,
            // then falls away and levels off at mid deck 11 (h = -1.3930, reached at r = 1.20)
            quad -6.0084, -0.5899 to -5.9297, -0.5899
            line to -5.6000, -0.5899
            quad -5.4164, -0.5899 to -5.2500, -0.5580
            quad -4.7624, -0.4647 to -4.3000, -0.4647
            quad -3.6132, -0.4571 to -3.0000, -0.6392
            quad -2.5506, -0.7906 to -2.1000, -1.0048
            quad -1.7238, -1.1712 to -1.3500, -1.3381
            quad -1.2270, -1.3930 to -1.0924, -1.3930
    
            // flat along mid deck 11, then square down the leading wall of deck 11
            line to -0.9450, -1.3930
            line to -0.9450, -1.4635
    
            // a pronounced turn from that wall into the floor of deck 11
            quad -0.9450, -1.5335 to -0.8450, -1.5335
            line to -0.8150, -1.5335
    
            // the rib: a U as thick as it is deep, its right side turning horizontal where the
            // dome starts, and that horizontal running in to the axis
            line to -0.8150, -1.5710
            quad -0.7775, -1.6460 to -0.7400, -1.5710
            line to -0.7400, -1.5335
            line to  0.0000, -1.5335
            close
        }
    }
    egg {
        radii 1.56, 1.17
        scale X 0.5
        rotate Z -90
        translate [-0.2, 0.30775, 0]
    }
    lathe {
        path {
            // the bridge: a flat roof, a quarter-elliptical corner (two quads, one per 45 degrees)
            // and a straight front wall.  The corner leaves the roof where the cutaway has it, 0.215
            // in from the wall, and meets the wall a third of the way up from the egg at the bow;
            // the cutaway's own corner is a circle meeting the wall at 1.16, but the egg sits lower
            // than the drawn deck there, and a circle would leave too much wall showing.
            move to 0, 1.3750
            line to -0.7640, 1.3750
            quad -0.8531, 1.3750 to -0.9160, 1.2986
            quad -0.9790, 1.2221 to -0.9790, 1.1140

            // the wall runs on down into the egg: the egg's top falls as low as 0.935 under the
            // bridge's rim, so stopping at the drawing's deck (1.09) would leave a gap
            line to -0.9790, 0.8500
            line to  0.0000, 0.8500
            close
        }
    }
    lathe {
        // the upper sensor dome: a spherical cap on the bridge roof, in the proportion to the bridge
        // that the cutaway draws the two (base 0.644, rise 0.156).  Its foot sinks a little into
        // the roof so the two never share a surface.
        path {
            move to 0, 1.5314
            quad -0.1680, 1.5314 to -0.3312, 1.4918
            quad -0.4945, 1.4521 to -0.6437, 1.3750
            line to -0.6437, 1.3600
            line to  0.0000, 1.3600
            close
        }
        material {
            pigment CosmicLatte
        }
    }
    lathe {
        // the lower sensor dome: a spherical cap hanging inside the rib, starting right where the
        // rib's inner side turns horizontal (r = 0.740), and as deep for its width as the cutaway
        // draws it (0.162).  Its top rises a little into the hull so the two never share a surface.
        path {
            move to 0, -1.5200
            line to -0.7400, -1.5200
            line to -0.7400, -1.5335
            quad -0.5659, -1.6135 to -0.3787, -1.6544
            quad -0.1916, -1.6953 to 0, -1.6953
            close
        }
        material {
            pigment CosmicLatte
        }
    }
    cylinder {
        // the bridge's elevator housing: at the back of the bridge, its centre 98% of the way out
        // to the bridge's rim as the cutaway draws it, so it straddles the bridge's aft wall; its
        // top stands just proud of the roof, and its foot is buried in the egg (whose top is 1.025
        // at the lowest under it)
        min Y 0.85
        max Y 1.385
        scale [0.183, 1, 0.183]
        translate [0.960, 0, 0]
    }
    // The windows on the housing under the bridge, as FJ's top view and profile draw them and the studio model
    // has them, all lit.  A row of five each side high on the housing, just out from the bridge: 0.15 along
    // the curve (the aft one 0.11) and 0.08 across it, 1.22 from the bridge's axis -- as far out from the
    // bridge's wall as FJ's top view draws them out from his -- at 50, 61, 73, 85 and 105 degrees round
    // from dead ahead.  Below them, round ports each 0.06 across: three along each side and a close pair aft
    // of them, and a pair on the housing's front either side of the centerline; FJ draws the housing taller
    // than the cutaway this one is built from, so their heights are his, in proportion to its side.  Each is
    // cut from the housing made a little fuller, so it lies on the curve, standing a hair proud: the row by
    // its outline as seen from above, the ports along the housing's own normal.
    intersection {
        egg {
            radii 1.56, 1.17
            scale 1.003
            scale X 0.5
            rotate Z -90
            translate [-0.2, 0.30775, 0]
        }
        union {
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y -40.20  translate [-0.7875, 0.8000, 0.9318] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y -28.70  translate [-0.5859, 0.8000, 1.0701] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y -16.70  translate [-0.3506, 0.8000, 1.1685] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y -5.05  translate [-0.1074, 0.8000, 1.2153] }
            cube { scale [0.0550, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y 15.40  translate [0.3240, 0.8000, 1.1762] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y 220.20  translate [-0.7875, 0.8000, -0.9318] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y 208.70  translate [-0.5859, 0.8000, -1.0701] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y 196.70  translate [-0.3506, 0.8000, -1.1685] }
            cube { scale [0.0750, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y 185.05  translate [-0.1074, 0.8000, -1.2153] }
            cube { scale [0.0550, 0.5000, 0.0400]  rotate Y 164.60  translate [0.3240, 0.8000, -1.1762] }
        }
        material {
            pigment [1, 0.76, 0.31]         // a yellow warm white, (1.0, 0.88, 0.59) as it shows under one white light
            ambient 1
            diffuse 0
            specular 0
        }
    }
    intersection {
        egg {
            radii 1.56, 1.17
            scale 1.003
            scale X 0.5
            rotate Z -90
            translate [-0.2, 0.30775, 0]
        }
        union {
            cylinder { min Y -0.08  max Y 0.08  scale [0.0300, 1, 0.0300]  rotate X 51.05  rotate Y -31.70  translate [-0.9600, 0.6000, 1.2306] }
            cylinder { min Y -0.08  max Y 0.08  scale [0.0300, 1, 0.0300]  rotate X 36.46  rotate Y -3.13  translate [0.0000, 0.7550, 1.3201] }
