    // The windows round the saucer's rim and on its underside, where FJ's bottom view and profile draw them
    // and as large -- all but one group, which is the studio model's.  On the rim's raked face, twice each
    // side, is a row of four with a fifth below it and a little beyond.  The aft group is FJ's, 129-135
    // degrees round from dead ahead: 0.12 across and 0.078 tall, high on the face, with the fifth smaller.
    // The forward group is as the studio model's photograph from below the starboard bow has it, 37-47
    // degrees round: lower on the face (the row's middle 0.195 below the rim's edge, the fifth's 0.356),
    // farther apart, and all five 0.172 across and 0.077 tall.  At the bow are three ovals 0.30 across and
    // 0.27 tall; three more, 0.12 by 0.09, on the flat just inside the rim, 116-119 degrees round; and
    // further in, on each side a pair and an arc of five, with a row of seven across the centerline ahead of
    // the lower dome and a row of three outside it.  The photograph shows which are lit -- all but the aft
    // window of each pair and the outer two of the seven -- and that the middle of each arc is no window but
    // a strip painted gold (measured against the plating beside it).  It also shows windows where FJ draws
    // none: round lit ports 0.06 across, six on the underside and two on the rim's face, and a small dark
    // window between each arc and pair.  Its windows are found by casting its rays back onto the hull
    // through a camera fitted to it -- corrected, on the rim, by where it puts the saucer's edge -- and
    // mirrored to port.  Each is a plate cut from the hull's surface moved a hair out -- the rim's face
